# TI-59/PC-100A Program: Calendar Printer, by Lou Cargile
#
# From 52-Notes, V3N5p3. This is a series of calendar programs that started
# with this issue of 52-Notes. The issue contained three programs, and later
# editions kept adding faster and more compact programs. Our goal is to collect
# and preserve them here in the form of preload files.
#
# This is the documentation given for this program in the original article:
#
# Algorithm:
# 1. Determine whether to print 1 month or balance of specified year.
# 2. Call ML-20 D with 1st day of the specified month and year to get
#    day of week for 1st day; convert returned 1,2,...6,0 to 1,2,...7.
# 3. Check for leap year, Feb, odd or even month, July, Aug, to get
#    number of days per month.
# 4. Print month header.
# 5. Use the calculated day of week to determine where to begin filling
#    print buffers from a quasi base seven counter; print filled buffers
#    and refill from the counter until all days have been printed.
# 6. For more than one month: Repeat steps 2-5, incrementing the month
#    until all months for the specified year have been printed.
#
# User Instructions: For one month: Key MM.YYYY, press B; for balance
# of year: Key MM.YYYY, press A.
#
# Transcribed from a compact magazine/manual program listing (OCR'd), same
# shorthand dialect as examples/calendar-01-Weinberger.ti59 (see the
# calc-u-59-statefiles skill's "52-Notes" section) plus flag operations:
# Stflg<n> = 2nd Stflg (set flag n), Ifflg<n> <dest> = 2nd Ifflg (if flag n,
# go to dest). "/" in the source is the division key (55) -- a handful of
# OCR'd "+" signs in this listing were actually "/" and have already been
# corrected to "/" before this transcription.
# Program length reconstructed step-by-step and cross-checked against the
# step numbers printed at the start of every listing line (000, 023, 056,
# 083, 108, 131, 159, 188, 214, 238, 264, 288, 314, 339, 364) -- every
# line's decoded length matches its declared next-line boundary exactly.
# Notable OCR resolutions:
#   - "xXt" is ambiguous between EQ (x=t) and X<>T (exchange, keycode 32)
#     the same as in calendar-01; resolved per-occurrence by what's
#     syntactically possible (EQ needs a valid destination token right
#     after it; when the next token can't be one -- e.g. "R8", a bare
#     small digit with no address shape, or nothing before a non-numeric
#     token -- it must be X<>T instead).
#   - "S07"/"SO" = STO 07 / STO 0 (same STO/RCL-with-2-digit-operand
#     convention as calendar-01); a bare "O" is a plain digit 0 throughout,
#     including the "rtn O S14" around step 331.
#   - The two "Dsz" instructions around step 289-311 both take a plain
#     (direct) register operand -- 07 and 13 respectively -- not indirect;
#     the second one's destination (332) lands entirely within its own
#     line, with no spillover into the next.
#   - "...345 rtn O S14 Stflg3 rtn rtn" (around step 331) is exactly as
#     printed: two separate RTNs at the end, not a duplicate.
#   - Decimal literals (".01", ".08", ".13", ".02", ".88") all cost 3 steps
#     each (2nd/./ key, then each digit separately) -- confirmed uniformly
#     by two lines' checksums, so no digit-omission shortcuts apply here.
# Uses Master Library program 20 (day-of-week helper), hence
# SOLID-STATE-MODULE: ML below.

MODEL: TI-59
SOLID-STATE-MODULE: ML
PRINTER: on

CUECARD:
Template: MagnetCard
Title: Calendar Printer
Banks: 1,2
Row1: Format: MM.YYYY
Row1Align: left
A: YEAR
B: 1 MONTH

PROGRAM:
01 02 32 43 08 22 77 00 10 91 01 44 06 61 00 26 76 11 22 86   # 000-019
04 22 86 00 42 06 43 06 42 00 59 42 08 22 44 00 65 01 00 00   # 020-039
85 01 95 44 00 43 06 22 59 65 01 00 00 00 00 95 42 10 43 00   # 040-059
36 20 14 42 07 00 32 43 07 22 67 00 76 07 44 07 43 10 55 04   # 060-079
95 22 59 22 67 00 89 86 00 03 01 42 13 00 32 43 08 55 02 95   # 080-099
22 59 22 67 01 34 43 08 32 02 22 67 01 29 02 09 42 13 22 87   # 100-119
00 01 45 01 44 13 61 01 45 07 32 61 01 38 43 08 32 07 22 77   # 120-139
01 45 01 44 13 43 08 85 04 07 95 42 09 69 00 73 09 69 04 43   # 140-159
10 69 06 71 03 68 43 46 69 01 43 45 69 02 43 44 69 03 43 43   # 160-179
69 04 69 05 71 03 69 22 86 01 22 86 03 01 42 00 00 42 14 42   # 180-199
16 05 42 15 22 86 02 43 14 71 02 89 43 14 44 16 71 02 36 87   # 200-219
02 02 04 71 02 36 87 02 02 04 71 02 36 61 02 04 97 15 02 75   # 220-239
43 16 59 84 00 69 20 43 00 32 05 22 67 02 66 01 42 00 69 05   # 240-259
86 02 87 03 02 81 43 16 22 59 42 16 05 42 15 01 00 00 49 16   # 260-279
92 98 22 87 04 02 88 91 81 97 07 03 38 87 01 03 04 93 00 01   # 280-299
44 14 86 01 93 00 01 44 14 22 97 13 03 32 43 14 22 59 32 93   # 300-319
00 08 67 03 39 93 01 03 67 03 45 92 00 42 14 86 03 92 92 93   # 320-339
00 02 44 14 92 93 08 08 44 14 43 14 59 32 01 67 03 59 92 44   # 340-359
14 92 76 12 86 04 61 00 21 43 47 69 01 69 02 69 03 69 04 69   # 360-379
05 92                                                         # 380-381

REGISTERS:
43 = 21000036
44 = 4300003700
45 = 370000
